<main class="guide wrap" id="main">
  <header class="docs-header">
    <h1>Create H5P content with ChatGPT, Claude or Grok</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An AI chat can write a whole H5P activity, not only its questions: a quiz, flash cards, a
      course presentation or any of <a href="#types">{{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types</a>. Here
      is how, from giving your chat the skill to the <code>.h5p</code> file in your Moodle.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chat needs the h5p-author skill, the instructions that tell it how to write H5P: you
      <a href="#install">give it to your chat</a>, once where the chat keeps skills, or with your
      message where it keeps none. Then the chat writes the activity. A chat whose code can run
      the build tool makes it a <code>.h5p</code> file itself, and for any other chat
      <a href="/h5p/build/">the build page</a> makes the file in your browser. The skill
      and the page are free and <a href="#open-source">open source</a>, with no account.
    </p>
    <p><a class="button button-solid button-large" href="/h5p/build/">Open the build page</a></p>
  </header>

  {#- The steps go by what a chat has, not by its name (the owner, 2026-11-20): what a chat does
      changes faster than a guide is read, and a step that names a chat then sends its reader the
      wrong way. Grok imported the zip under Skills on 2026-11-13 and had no place for one on
      2026-11-15, and a chat app for local models was added to this step and taken out within
      three days. So each step names what a reader can look for in their own chat (a place for
      skills, plugins, projects, code that runs) and says what to do with it and without it.
      No step says what one product does, and none says what a product lacks. The chats in the
      title are examples of an AI chat: the page says nothing of any of them alone.

      Seen so far, for this record and not for the page: Claude's Skills took h5p-author.zip and
      ChatGPT's Plugins the plugin zip (the owner, 2026-11-13); Grok read the skill from the zip
      attached to a message (2026-11-15); the archive kept in a ChatGPT Project built there on
      the owner's free account (2026-11-14, #49).

      The steps are drawn as the skill page's timeline (site.css). The zips are written by
      vite.config.ts from the release the site pins. Each keeps its address, which other pages
      link to, and its link here saves it under a name with the release in it, so a person can
      tell two downloads apart. The first step's heading keeps the id that the top bar and the
      skill's page link to.

      An agent with a terminal comes before the cases, and outside them: the cases are taken in
      order, and such an agent has no settings to look in, so it would stop at the last, the
      zip attached. The plugin zip is one layout of several, a `plugin.json` at its root and the
      skill under `skills/`, the one ChatGPT's Plugins took on 2026-11-13 (vite.config.ts): a
      chat whose plugins ask for another refuses it, which the case says, and the next takes
      it from there. -#}
  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">1</span></span> Give your chat the skill</h2>
    <p>
      The skill is a small zip of text files. AI chats change what they offer from month to
      month, so this guide goes by what your chat has, not by its name.
    </p>
    <p>
      In an agent with a terminal, one that runs commands on your computer, with Node.js
      installed, run
      <code>npx skills add missing-elements/h5p-toolkit --skill h5p-author</code>, and go on to
      step 2. If the command does not list your agent, or fails, use a zip as below. In a chat
      app, look in its settings, and take the first of these that fits:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It has a place for skills.</strong> Download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author.zip" download="h5p-author-{{ skill.version }}.zip">h5p-author-{{ skill.version }}.zip</a>
        and upload it there, once: the chat keeps it for every activity you ask for. If the
        settings have a switch for running code, turn it on, since the chat builds the file with it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It has plugins, and no skills.</strong> Download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author-chatgpt.zip" download="h5p-author-chatgpt-{{ skill.version }}.zip">h5p-author-chatgpt-{{ skill.version }}.zip</a>,
        the same skill laid out as a plugin, and upload it there, once. Plugins come in more
        than one layout: a chat that refuses this one takes the zip attached, as the next case says.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It has neither, or it refuses the upload.</strong> Attach
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author.zip" download="h5p-author-{{ skill.version }}.zip">h5p-author-{{ skill.version }}.zip</a>
        to the message where you ask. The chat reads the skill from the zip, and a new chat needs
        it attached again.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The zips hold h5p-author {{ skill.version }}, the release the build page runs: text files and
      nothing that runs, which you can read
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-toolkit/tree/main/skills/h5p-author">on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="ask-heading">
    <h2 id="ask-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">2</span></span> Ask your chat</h2>
    <p>Open a new chat and paste this prompt with your own subject:</p>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P activity on [your subject] for [your students].</pre>
    <p>
      Name the type if you know it, such as "an H5P Question Set of ten questions": the
      <a href="#types">types</a> and <a href="#prompts">prompts to start from</a> are below. For
      another language, say so, such as "in French": the activity's buttons come in it too, where
      H5P has a translation.
    </p>
    <p>
      To change an activity you already have, attach its <code>.h5p</code> and say what to change,
      such as "add two harder questions". A chat that builds the file itself, as in step 4, reads
      the activity back, changes it and builds it again, if it was made with the versions of H5P's
      libraries the build tool has. Otherwise the chat says so and offers to make the activity anew.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
    <h2 id="answer-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">3</span></span> Answer its question</h2>
    <p>
      If the prompt names no type, the chat proposes one or two that fit your material, and you
      choose. It asks nothing else, and writes the activity. To put your name and a licence on it,
      say them in the prompt, such as "by Ann Lee, CC BY 4.0"; otherwise it carries the name the
      chat knows you by, or "Unknown", and an undisclosed licence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="file-heading">
    <h2 id="file-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">4</span></span> Get the .h5p file</h2>
    <p>
      The build tool is a Node.js program, so what the chat hands you depends on what its code
      can do:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the <code>.h5p</code> file itself, where the chat's code runs Node.js and gets the build tool: it downloads the tool, or takes it from <a href="#archive">the build archive</a>, below;</li>
      <li>or its source, as a file or a block of JSON, where the chat runs no code, or its code cannot get the tool: open or paste it <a href="/h5p/build/#build-it">on the build page</a> and choose Build.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      From the source, the build page builds it in your browser, plays it, and saves the file
      with Download the .h5p. A chat that built the file gives its source file too: open it on the
      build page to play the activity before you use it.
    </p>
    {#- The archive is offered here, where a reader has just seen which of the two their chat
        gave, and not in step 1: nobody can tell beforehand whether a chat's code downloads the
        tool, and the skill's own reply offers the archive where it would help (route 4's fixed
        line). A project is any chat's, not one product's: a chat that has projects keeps their
        files for every chat in them, and the skill looks for the archive among the chat's files
        wherever it came from (route 1). Where a project does not hand its files to the chat's
        code, the reader attaches it, which the step says too. -#}
    <p id="archive">
      A chat that runs code and still hands over the source could not download the build tool,
      and its reply says so. Give it the tool yourself: download
      <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a>, the
      build archive, once, and put it where the chat finds it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your chat has projects.</strong> Add the archive to a project, and ask in a chat of that project: it is then there for every activity.</li>
      <li><strong>It has none, or the chat does not find the archive there.</strong> Attach it to the message where you ask.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The archive holds the same release's build tool, which runs in the chat's own sandbox, and
      the H5P libraries it puts in the file, each under its own licence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="check-heading">
    <h2 id="check-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">5</span></span> Play it and check it</h2>
    <p>
      Work through all of it, wrong answers included, and check the answers and feedback against
      your material: the build checks the structure, not whether the content is right.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the build lists problems, choose Copy the problems for the chat and paste them into the
      chat that wrote the source. It fixes them all and writes the source again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="course-heading">
    <h2 id="course-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">6</span></span> Add it to your course</h2>
    <p>
      In Moodle, switch Edit mode on in your course, choose Add an activity or resource, then H5P,
      and upload the file under Package file. The file carries the H5P libraries it needs, but by
      default Moodle installs those it lacks only for a manager or an administrator: if it says a
      library is missing, see <a href="#moodle-libraries">below</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Any other H5P site takes it where it adds H5P content, H5P.com too, and Lumi opens it on your
      computer. With no H5P site, h5p-offline-player plays it
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-toolkit/blob/main/docs/setup.md">on a site of your own</a>
      or <a href="https://h5p-offline-player.vercel.app/app/">offline, in its app</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prompts">
    <h2 id="prompts">Prompts to start from</h2>
    <p>
      Each names its type, so the chat asks nothing. Change the subject
      and the numbers to yours.
    </p>

    <h3>A course presentation</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the course presentation prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Course Presentation of six slides on writing learning objectives with Bloom's taxonomy, with a question on each slide.</pre>
    <p>
      Claude Haiku made the sample on the build page from this request:
      <a href="/h5p/build/">play it there</a>, or <a href="/h5p/build/sample.h5p" download>download it</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>A quiz</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the quiz prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Question Set of ten multiple-choice questions on the water cycle for 11-year-olds, with feedback that explains each answer.</pre>

    <h3>Flash cards</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the flash cards prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make H5P Dialog Cards for twenty common Spanish verbs: the verb on the front, and its meaning and an example sentence on the back.</pre>

    <h3>Fill in the blanks, from your text</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the fill in the blanks prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Fill in the Blanks from the text below, with its key terms as the blanks.

[Paste your text here]</pre>

    <h3>An interactive book, from your notes</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the interactive book prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Interactive Book from my lesson notes below: a chapter for each topic, each ending with two questions.

[Paste your notes here]</pre>

    <h3>A branching scenario</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the branching scenario prompt">Use the h5p-author skill to make an H5P Branching Scenario in which a new team lead deals with a project running late: three decisions, each with a better and a worse choice, and feedback at every end.</pre>
  </section>

  {#- The types the skill's table offers, in the release the site runs, by their names in H5P's
      editor (src/_data/skill.js): those the chat writes whole, then those that play the user's
      media. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="types">
    <h2 id="types">The {{ skill.offered.length }} H5P content types it offers</h2>
    <p>
      The chat offers these, from the h5p-author skill's list. Name one in your prompt, or say what
      you want and let it propose one.
    </p>

    <h3>From text alone</h3>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | rejectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}.</dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>

    <h3>With your pictures, video or recordings</h3>
    <p>
      Attach your files to the chat, or tell it their names, and it writes the activity around
      them. The build page then asks you to choose them from your computer.
    </p>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | selectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}. <span class="type-media">You add {{ type.media }}.</span></dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
  </section>

  {#- What most people ask first, which the header answers in a word and this section in full. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="open-source">
    <h2 id="open-source">Free and open source</h2>
    <p>
      The build page and the h5p-author skill are free, need no account, and are open source under
      the MIT licence. The build page's code is in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/missing-elements.github.io/tree/main/src/h5p/build">this site's repository</a>,
      and the skill and the player are in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-toolkit">h5p-toolkit</a>. The H5P
      runtime the player loads is <a href="/h5p/assets/runtime-LICENSE.txt">GPL-3.0</a>, and the H5P
      libraries in a file are each under their own licence, most MIT, as
      <a href="/h5p/assets/libraries-LICENSES.txt">the pack's licence list</a> says. The packages
      bundled into the build page and into the build archive's tool are listed with their licences
      in <a href="/h5p/assets/build-page-LICENSES.txt">the page's list</a> and
      <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-LICENSES.txt">the tool's</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Privacy first: nothing you make is sent to this site, and there is no server of ours in
      between. The chat builds the file in its own sandbox, or the build page
      builds and plays it in your browser. Your AI chat sees what you send it, as it sees
      anything you ask it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="local">
    <h2 id="local">With a local model, or offline</h2>
    <p>
      A chat app for local models writes the activity as any chat does. What it hands you
      depends on its code, as in <a href="#file-heading">step 4</a>: where the app runs no code,
      or its code is not Node.js, the chat hands over the source, and the build page builds the
      file.
    </p>
    <p>
      With Node.js 22 or later on your own computer, the whole build can stay offline. Install the
      skill in an agent with a terminal that runs a local model, from Ollama or LM Studio, and download
      <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a> once.
      Then tell the agent where the archive is. It builds the <code>.h5p</code> file on your
      computer, with no internet and nothing sent anywhere.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="help">
    <h2 id="help">If something goes wrong</h2>

    <h3>The chat doesn't use the skill</h3>
    <p>
      Name it in your message, as the prompts here do: "Use the h5p-author skill to…". If the chat
      knows no such skill, check that it is still where <a href="#install">step 1</a> put it, or
      attached to your message, and ask in a new chat.
    </p>

    <h3>The chat says it cannot get the build tool</h3>
    <p>
      Download <a href="/h5p/bundles/h5p-author-build.tar.gz" download>h5p-author-build.tar.gz</a>
      and attach it to your next message: a chat whose code runs Node.js builds the file from it,
      without installing or downloading anything. If your chat has projects, keep the archive in
      one and ask in a chat of it: there is then nothing to attach again. A chat whose code runs
      no Node.js gives you the activity's source for the build page instead.
    </p>

    <h3>The build lists problems</h3>
    <p>
      Choose Copy the problems for the chat, and paste them into the chat that wrote the source. It
      fixes them all and writes the source again: paste that in place of the old one, and build
      again.
    </p>

    <h3>The chat's link builds nothing</h3>
    <p>
      Ask the chat for the source as JSON instead, and paste it on the build page. The skill
      gives the build page's plain address: a link that carries the activity breaks where a chat
      writes one character of it wrong.
    </p>

    <h3 id="moodle-libraries">Moodle says a library is missing</h3>
    <p>
      The file carries every library it needs, but by default Moodle lets only managers and
      administrators install new ones. Ask one to upload the file in Site administration, under
      Manage H5P content types: after that, files with the same types work for you too.
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

